{
 "about": "The looks: film stocks and movie grades in the Print model, each with its own tone, colour and texture. Built by scripts/filmlooks.py; exported with scripts/print-look.py export-swift. Keys left out inherit from Print. grey = display luminance of 18% grey; slope = log-log contrast below the shoulder; shoulder = how hard highlights turn; white/floor = display white and black; printer/crossover = [red, blue] or [red, green, blue] log-exposure offsets (+ = less of that colour) / [red, blue] gamma splits; interlayer = saturation; channel_mix = 3x3 scene RGB mix (rows normalised to 1); crosstalk = 0..1 of print-dye density coupling; grain = density std at 4032 px; shadow_tint/highlight_tint = split toning, offsets on the encoded print output weighted (1-Y)^2 / Y^2 (made luminance-neutral by filmlooks.py). Looks stay far apart: nearest-neighbour mean ΔE (Oklab) >= 0.06 on the test images, Print included. hue_twists = film color by hue, [hue deg, width deg, rotate deg, saturation, stops] each (0 red, 60 yellow, 120 green, 180 cyan, 240 blue, 300 magenta; skin ~30, foliage ~80, sky ~225), on scene light in log space so neutrals are untouched; dye_saturation = saturation in print density (saturated colors get darker; < 0 drains them like retained silver); key_black = Technicolor's black key image of the green record (denser reds, blues, magentas).",
